{"author":{"id":"1949ffc23f11a8cb4f8c42a45c9ad241b49570df9a18687489e9ea78d87c54a3"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1779132376,"nonce":"QUI5OrBwdI3mZ34FDWyzQrXDUlo=","message":"## Consolidated Top-Level Spec: Scenario Projection Workspace\n\nThis comment consolidates the post-RLRMP UI review, the research sidecar, and the Claude/Gemini advisory passes into a single top-level direction for the next agents. It should be treated as the current product/design basis for this issue until superseded.\n\n### Executive Decision\n\nFeedbax Studio's top pane should become a **Scenario Projection Workspace**. The canonical user-authored object is a typed scenario document, not a set of disconnected graph nodes, bottom-pane forms, imported metadata rows, or canvas decorations.\n\nThe top pane renders that scenario through synchronized projections:\n\n- `Graph`: causal/computational structure, ports, wires, taps, probes, and graph bindings.\n- `Workspace`: physical/spatial/task structure, including task objects, mechanics objects, goals, targets, trajectories, perturbations, and later run/eval trajectory overlays.\n- `Objectives`: first-class relationship objects such as losses, metrics, constraints, rewards, probes, and regularizers.\n- `Inspector`: follows the selected scenario entity and provides contextual editing.\n\nThe bottom pane should remain the **Pipeline/Execution Workspace**. It owns stage selection, run/eval collections, execution targets, optimizer/batch/checkpoint settings, launch/readiness controls, logs, run tables, manifest/artifact inspection, and downstream stage actions. It should consume scenario snapshots rather than author structural state itself.\n\n### Primary Product Rule\n\nEverything visible that changes execution meaning must be a projection of durable scenario/spec state. Nothing important should exist only as canvas coordinates, bottom-pane metadata, local React state, static labels, or agent-authored descriptive text.\n\n### Important Naming Decision\n\nUse **Mechanics**, not Biomechanics, in the Studio UI and specs unless a specific object is biological. The same workspace model should support biological arms, mechanical plants, simple point masses, robots, and other physical systems.\n\n### Task and Mechanics Entity Decision\n\nTask and Mechanics should be **first-class scenario entities**, not only graph nodes.\n\nThe graph may contain nodes or chips that reference those entities, but authoritative task/mechanics parameters should live on scenario entities. This prevents a repeat of stale duplicated state, where a graph node, workspace view, and bottom-pane form each appear to own different versions of the same task or plant.\n\nRecommended first assumption:\n\n- A scenario has zero or one active task/workspace environment.\n- A scenario can have zero, one, or many mechanics objects.\n- Mechanics entities can bind to graph nodes/components.\n- The task behaves like the scenario environment: it owns unconditional inputs, targets, events, trial distributions, and objective/reward context.\n- Evaluation protocols are scenario/task variants or overrides, not static metadata cards.\n\n### Information Architecture\n\nTop pane shell:\n\n- Scenario chip/status area: active scenario, stage context, validation state, and optional scenario-tree toggle.\n- Projection tabs: `Graph`, `Workspace`, `Objectives`.\n- Main projection canvas/list: one active projection initially.\n- Inspector: one selection-driven editor, with pinning later.\n- Optional scenario tree/outliner: useful for navigation, but not the primary authoring surface in the first version.\n\nInitial implementation should use one active projection at a time. Two-column editor groups are desirable later, but should not be in the first slice. A one-column shell with fast projection switching will expose the model and selection problems earlier and with less UI complexity.\n\n### Selection Model\n\nThe product needs one scenario-selection model across the top pane:\n\n- Selecting a graph node, graph port, workspace object, objective term, or scenario-tree row selects the same canonical entity ID.\n- Hover and selection should cross-highlight related objects in other projections.\n- Inspector follows the selected entity by default.\n- Pinned inspectors can come later, but pinning must be explicit.\n- Bottom-pane run-table selection is a separate collection-selection model and must not silently fight with top-pane scenario selection.\n\nThis is the most important interaction architecture decision. Multiple silent selections were one of the concrete failure modes in the Train/Evaluate UI review.\n\n### Scenario Entity Model\n\nThe scenario document should eventually expose a normalized entity index with stable IDs and typed entity kinds such as:\n\n- `graph_node`\n- `graph_port`\n- `graph_edge`\n- `task_object`\n- `mechanics_object`\n- `objective_term`\n- `probe`\n- `metric`\n- `temporal_event`\n- `temporal_phase`\n- `stage_protocol`\n- `artifact_overlay`\n\nEntities and relationships should be edited through typed commands rather than ad hoc store writes. This enables undo, autosave, validation, issue reproduction, and deterministic lowering.\n\n### Projection Responsibilities\n\n#### Graph Projection\n\nGraph remains the causal/computational model view.\n\nExpected changes:\n\n- Task/Mechanics graph nodes become bindings/references to scenario entities when applicable.\n- Right-clicking ports, port labels, probes, taps, barnacles, state paths, or edges should expose actions such as `Inspect`, `Add objective`, `Add metric`, `Add probe`, `Show in Workspace`, and `Show data`.\n- Thick/full-state edges should open a state/path selector before the same actions are offered.\n- Ports/state paths referenced by objectives or metrics should get small relationship badges.\n- Double-click still makes sense for entering true subgraphs, but drilldown is not the primary model for task/mechanics authoring.\n\n#### Workspace Projection\n\nWorkspace is the spatial/task/mechanics projection.\n\nStart with a 2D/SVG reaching workspace, not a general 3D editor. It should be enough to show a reaching task, an effector, targets/goals, trajectories, perturbations, and objective links. A 3D or CAD-like editor can come later.\n\nExpected first objects:\n\n- Effectors and simple mechanics bodies.\n- Goal points and target distributions.\n- Trajectories or trajectory placeholders.\n- Workspace bounds and task regions where relevant.\n- Objective relationship overlays.\n- Later: artifact overlays for trained/evaluated trajectories.\n\nWorkspace interactions should create real scenario mutations. For example, connecting an end effector to a goal creates an objective term; it is not merely a drawn line.\n\n#### Objectives Projection\n\nObjectives are first-class relationship objects. They should not be hidden inside Train-tab form rows.\n\nThe Objectives projection should be a compact model-explorer/list over objective terms, grouped by role or phase as needed. It should support:\n\n- Selecting an objective term and cross-highlighting its participants.\n- Editing weight, norm, temporal selector, role, and enable/disable state.\n- Opening the full objective dialog for heavier edits.\n- Creating non-spatial objectives such as regularizers.\n- Deleting or disabling objective terms.\n\nThe visual overlays in Graph and Workspace are projections of these terms.\n\n### Objective/Loss Authoring Contract\n\nAuthoring should converge on a generalized `ObjectiveTermSpec`, with current executable `LossTermSpec` as a lowering target rather than the primary UI model.\n\nA generalized objective term should include:\n\n- Stable ID and label.\n- Role: loss, metric, constraint, reward, regularizer, probe, etc.\n- Source selector.\n- Target selector or target value.\n- Operator/relationship: distance, difference, energy, tracking, endpoint error, barrier, cross-entropy, etc.\n- Norm/penalty/aggregation: squared L2, L2, L1, Huber, RMSE, custom registered objective, etc.\n- Temporal selector and aggregation level.\n- Weight or schedule.\n- Units, coordinate frame, shape/dtype expectations where needed.\n- Validation and lowering status.\n\nAuthoring entry points:\n\n1. Workspace: select/drag from effector to goal or trajectory, then confirm objective type, components, temporal phase, norm, and weight.\n2. Graph: right-click a port/state/probe/edge and choose `Add objective from here`; the source selector is prefilled.\n3. Objectives: create a term directly when the objective is abstract, regularizing, or not naturally spatial.\n4. Inspector: from any selected state/entity, show related objectives and actions to focus, edit, disable, or remove them.\n\n### Selector Contract\n\nStudio needs typed selector references rather than only compact strings. Strings can remain as serialization, but the canonical UI/spec form should carry enough information to validate and cross-highlight selectors.\n\nSelectors should be able to reference:\n\n- Graph nodes, ports, and edges.\n- Taps, probes, and barnacles.\n- State/PyTree paths.\n- Exposed component fields/parameters.\n- Task goals, trajectories, cue events, and trial fields.\n- Mechanics links, joints, muscles/actuators, effectors, coordinate frames, and workspace points.\n- Artifact fields, model checkpoints, evaluation outputs, trajectory arrays, and analysis outputs.\n\nSelectors should declare namespace, target path/ID, expected shape/dtype, units, coordinate frame, stage compatibility, and editability/observability where relevant.\n\n### Temporal Model\n\nTraining, evaluation, objectives, metrics, analysis, and playback must share one temporal vocabulary.\n\nThe scenario should include named phases/events and selectors such as:\n\n- Target onset, go cue, movement onset, hold onset, trial end.\n- Absolute step ranges where appropriate.\n- Event-relative windows.\n- Trial-aligned and run-aligned aggregation levels.\n- Schedules over time, trial, batch, epoch, curriculum phase, or evaluation condition.\n\nThis prevents loss terms, evaluation protocols, and analysis plots from using incompatible meanings of words like `final`, `movement`, or `pre-go`.\n\n### Stage and Inheritance Semantics\n\nStage context should determine what is editable, inherited, locked, or viewed as an artifact.\n\nTraining:\n\n- Edits the model graph, task/workspace, mechanics bindings, objectives/losses, metrics/probes, temporal selectors, and training-relevant scenario state.\n- Freezes a scenario snapshot at launch.\n- Bottom pane owns optimizer, batches, seeds, checkpoint cadence, compute target, launch/status/logs, and run collection operations.\n\nEvaluation:\n\n- Consumes selected training artifacts/checkpoints.\n- Inherits the training scenario by default, then allows explicit fork/override for evaluation conditions, perturbations, task variants, probes, and metrics.\n- Must show inherited vs overridden fields clearly.\n- Does not accidentally mutate the training scenario.\n\nAnalyze:\n\n- Consumes selected runs/evaluations/artifacts.\n- Long-term, analysis specs should be stage-owned typed specs over artifact/state selectors.\n- The current bottom-pane analysis graph can remain during migration, but should not become a disconnected black box.\n\nReport:\n\n- Consumes selected analysis/report products.\n- Owns report composition and product selection, not computation unless a report transform explicitly produces the product.\n\n### Sweeps, Protocols, Metrics, and Run Labels\n\nDo not hard-code sweeps, protocols, validation conditions, or training metrics as table metadata.\n\n- Sweep variants should come from explicit authored choices over task parameters, objective parameters, stage protocol fields, seeds/replicates, or templates.\n- Evaluation protocols should be scenario/task variants or overrides.\n- Training/evaluation metrics shown in run tables should come from objective/metric/analysis specs or task templates.\n- Run labels should be deterministic summaries of authored choices, with optional user rename support.\n- Imported legacy metrics should be visibly treated as imported fields, not implied to be live authored training metrics.\n\nThis links directly to `e5a1e6c`.\n\n### Imported Legacy Runs\n\nImported runs such as the RLRMP movement-ramp fixture should be wrapped in sparse or synthetic scenario/stage records:\n\n- Preserve whatever can be inferred: task family, mechanics, objective/loss fields, metrics, checkpoint/artifact paths, labels, and provenance.\n- Mark missing or imported-only fields explicitly.\n- Allow selection/filtering/evaluation/analysis/report workflows.\n- Do not imply the imported run was authored with full Studio scenario state if it was not.\n- Where possible, reconstruct a read-only Workspace view so the user can understand the pipeline without needing an agent to insert data.\n\n### Validation and Lowering\n\nThe scenario authoring model should lower deterministically to executable Feedbax specs/manifests.\n\nNeeded contracts:\n\n- Frontend scenario specs are the durable source of truth for user-authored domain state.\n- Backend validators check scenario, task, mechanics, objective, selector, temporal, and stage protocol compatibility.\n- Lowering produces current executable structures such as graph specs, task specs, training specs, loss specs, eval specs, analysis specs, and manifests.\n- Validation errors attach to visible entities and spec paths.\n- Launching a run freezes the scenario snapshot and lowered execution specs into the manifest.\n- Later edits create a new draft/version, not silent mutation of executed history.\n\n### Precedent Patterns to Import\n\nUse these patterns selectively rather than copying any one product:\n\n- Unity/Unreal: one selection model across viewport/tree/details, with an inspector that follows selection.\n- Houdini: panes follow selection by default but can be pinned explicitly.\n- Fusion/CAE tools: constraints/relationships are first-class selectable/editable objects.\n- MathWorks Requirements Toolbox: relationship badges, traceability, and impact highlighting.\n- Blender Viewer Node: inspect intermediate node outputs without turning every preview into permanent graph structure.\n- Foxglove/RViz-style tools: spatial overlays and linked panels for live/recorded trajectory inspection.\n- W\u0026B/Neptune: useful for bottom-pane run tables and artifact browsing, not structural authoring.\n\n### Implementation Order Decision\n\nImplement this in the following order. Future agents should bulk out child issues from these slices rather than starting with visual polish.\n\n#### Slice 1: Scenario Entity Registry, Selection Bus, and Inspector\n\nPurpose: create the foundation that all projections share.\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- Introduce or formalize a normalized scenario entity registry over existing graph/task/loss/probe state.\n- Add a top-pane selection store: selected entity IDs, hovered entity ID, active projection, active stage/scenario, and later pinned inspector ID.\n- Convert the right inspector to render from selected entity kind instead of from graph-only assumptions.\n- Ensure graph node selection writes canonical entity IDs.\n- Keep bottom-pane collection/run selection separate.\n\nAcceptance:\n\n- Selecting a graph node/port/entity drives the same inspector path future Workspace/Objectives will use.\n- The selection model can represent graph nodes, task objects, mechanics objects, and objective terms even if some are placeholders.\n\n#### Slice 2: Projection Shell and Read-Only Scenario Workspace\n\nPurpose: establish the mental model without risky authoring changes.\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- Add top-pane projection tabs: `Graph`, `Workspace`, `Objectives`.\n- Keep Graph as the current canvas.\n- Add a read-only 2D Workspace projection for the reaching example: effector, target/goal, simple trajectory/condition indicators where available.\n- Add an Objectives projection listing current/lowered objective terms.\n- Preserve selection across projection switches.\n\nAcceptance:\n\n- A user can select a related object/term and see corresponding context in another projection.\n- The Workspace view explains task/mechanics structure without dumping backend identifiers.\n\n#### Slice 3: Objective Authoring and Canonical Objective Terms\n\nPurpose: make the first genuinely integrated structural authoring workflow.\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- Promote authoring around `ObjectiveTermSpec` or equivalent.\n- Lower objectives to current executable loss/metric specs at run launch/validation time.\n- Add graph port/state action: `Add objective from here`.\n- Add workspace source-target objective creation for effector-to-goal and effector-to-trajectory cases.\n- Add objective badges/overlays on referenced graph/workspace entities.\n- Add objective edit/delete/disable in Objectives projection and inspector.\n\nAcceptance:\n\n- A user-created workspace objective appears in Objectives, marks the relevant graph/workspace entities, validates, and lowers to executable training loss state.\n\n#### Slice 4: Task/Mechanics as Scenario Entities and Graph Bindings\n\nPurpose: remove duplicated ownership and make authoring general.\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- Promote task/workspace and mechanics/plant concepts into scenario entities.\n- Bind graph nodes/components to those scenario entities rather than duplicating parameters.\n- Inspector shows binding state and navigation between graph reference and scenario entity.\n- Introduce inherited/overridden/locked markers needed by evaluation scenarios.\n\nAcceptance:\n\n- Editing a task/mechanics parameter updates the scenario entity and all projections; no bottom-pane or graph-node clone silently diverges.\n\n#### Slice 5: Bottom-Pane Scenario Consumption and Stage Protocol Cleanup\n\nPurpose: connect structural authoring to Train/Evaluate without returning to metadata dumps.\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- Train stage consumes active scenario snapshot and stage protocol.\n- Training protocol editor owns optimizer, batches, seeds, checkpoint cadence, compute target, and launch controls.\n- Evaluate stage composes selected training artifacts with inherited/forked scenario variants.\n- Run tables derive labels, metrics, and columns from authored scenario/stage specs where possible.\n- Imported legacy runs appear as sparse/imported manifests with explicit missing fields.\n\nAcceptance:\n\n- Train/Evaluate no longer hard-code sweeps, protocols, validation conditions, or metric columns as fake metadata.\n- Run/evaluation selection is interactive and stage-scoped.\n\n#### Slice 6: Richer Workspace, Metrics, Analysis, and Report Integration\n\nPurpose: deepen capabilities after the core product model is proven.\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- More task editing: target distributions, cue timings, trajectories, perturbations, trial variants.\n- More mechanics editing: links, joints, effectors, muscles/actuators, coordinate frames, constraints.\n- Metric specs from analysis/objective templates, including task-aware default metric sets.\n- Live or recorded trajectory overlays in Workspace.\n- Analysis specs move toward typed stage-owned selectors over artifact collections.\n- Report consumes analysis products and exposes composition/provenance.\n- Optional two-column top-pane layout once projections are useful enough to view side by side.\n\nAcceptance:\n\n- The app feels like one integrated authoring/execution environment rather than a graph canvas plus disconnected dashboards.\n\n### Explicit Non-Goals for the First Child Slice\n\nDo not start with:\n\n- A full 3D mechanics editor.\n- Two-column editor-group layout.\n- Full sweep authoring.\n- Full analysis/report migration.\n- Live trajectory streaming.\n- Complete imported-run reconstruction.\n- Visual redesign of every bottom-pane stage.\n\nThese are important, but they depend on the entity registry, selection bus, inspector, and projection shell.\n\n### Child-Issue Plan for Later Agent\n\nA later agent should create/bulk out child issues roughly matching the slices above, plus links back to:\n\n- `0c46f69` for bottom-pane pipeline responsibilities.\n- `f0ae3ce` for immediate Train/Evaluate run collection UI follow-through.\n- `e5a1e6c` for task-aware metrics.\n- `c6c6da0` for manifests/provider/lowering contracts.\n- `fa3978f` for cross-repo roadmap ordering.\n\nThe first implementation child should be Slice 1: scenario entity registry, selection bus, and inspector unification.\n\n---\nCo-Authored-By: Codex (GPT-5) \u003ccodex@openai.com\u003e","files":null}]}